-- title:
--   Order of J_H(M)[p] equals p^{h+toricRank}
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p \neq 2$ and a level $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` for $M/p \mid M$ is $1$; fix also a set $S$ of natural numbers, an algebraically closed field $K$ that is an algebra over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, and the Hecke–diamond input package `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`, which asserts `HeckeInputsHAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) M H ℓ` for every prime $\ell$ together with, for each $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\!\cdot\! F(\Gamma_H(M))$ satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d σ`. Let $\mathfrak{P}$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ in its nonunits, whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$, and assume $j$ lies in the $q$-expansion function field at level $SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$, so that the two-chart integral models over `R p` are available. The remaining data are summarised in four groups. Geometric data: an integral model $\mathfrak{X}$ of $X_H$ at $p$, level data $\Lambda$ and a Néron object $O$ for $J_H(M)$ at $\mathfrak{P}$, together with hypotheses that the designations built from $(\Lambda.X,\Lambda.f)$ and from $(O.G,O.g)$ with their identity sections represent the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by fibrewise algebraically trivial rigidified line bundles, over `R p` and after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$, separatedness of the base-changed curve, and compatibility of the two Poincaré bundles. Abel–Jacobi pinning: morphisms $ajQ$, $kQ$, $\overline{aj}$ and a section $\overline{\varepsilon}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x \cdot ajQ$ identified with the line bundle of the point divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the zero section, the compatibilities $kQ$ and $\overline{aj}$ satisfy, the requirement that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ be a homomorphism for the relative group law obtained from the representability datum, and the clause that for geometric points $x,s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ pinned to $\varepsilon^\infty$ there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[\,x\,]-[\,s\,]$ on places whose class is sent by $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to $x$ followed by $\overline{aj}$. Inertia ring: a henselian local discrete valuation domain $R$, with algebraically closed residue field and a faithful algebra map into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ landing in $\mathfrak{P}$, in which $p$ is irreducible, and which is exactly the subring of $\mathfrak{P}$ fixed by the inertia subgroup of $\mathfrak{P}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, that subgroup consisting precisely of the automorphisms fixing $R$ pointwise. Finite-part layer: a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R$ of height $h$ and an injective additive map $\Delta$ from its $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points to $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\!\cdot\! F_H)$ whose image at level $v$ is exactly $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$, which is equivariant for $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ acting through $R$-automorphisms, and which intertwines each Hecke or diamond generator `genOpH M H S g` with a compatible system of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal{G}$. Under these hypotheses the $p$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \overline{\mathbb{Q}}\!\cdot\! F_H)$, i.e. $\{x : p\,x = 0\}$, has cardinality $p^{\,h + O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$.
--
--   This is the order computation for the $p$-torsion of the Jacobian of $X_H(M)$ when $p$ exactly divides the level, in the form $p^{h+t}$ with $h$ the height of the finite (connected $p$-divisible) layer of the Néron object and $t$ its toric rank; it combines the characteristic-zero count $\#J[p]=p^{2g}$ with the Deligne–Rapoport genus identity $g+1=2g'+\#\mathrm{SS}$ and the identification of the finite part with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{G}[p]$. It is used by the downstream counting statements that compare the finite parts with reduced roots of the $\ell$-adic monodromy pairing and with spaces of regular differentials in the level-lowering argument.
